---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const P1 = `age ← 16
hasPass ← false

IF(age ≥ 18 OR hasPass)
{
  DISPLAY("Enter")
}
ELSE
{
  DISPLAY("Denied")
}`;

const P2 = `x ← 12
y ← 5

IF(NOT (x < y))
{
  DISPLAY("first")
}
IF(x > 10 AND y > 10)
{
  DISPLAY("second")
}
IF(x > 10 OR y > 10)
{
  DISPLAY("third")
}`;

const P3 = `local count = 0

if count then
    print("A")
end

if count == 0 then
    print("B")
end`;

const P4 = `n ← RANDOM(1, 4)
DISPLAY(n * 2)`;

const RefCard = `a ← expression           assign a copy of the result to a
DISPLAY(expression)      show the value, FOLLOWED BY A SPACE
INPUT()                  take a value from the user

+  -  *  /               17 / 5 is 3.4  (no truncation)
a MOD b                  remainder.  17 MOD 5 is 2
RANDOM(a, b)             whole number from a to b, BOTH INCLUDED

=  ≠  >  <  ≥  ≤         comparison.  = means EQUALS
NOT / AND / OR           boolean operators

IF(condition)            condition is TRUE or FALSE. Nothing else.
{ ... }                  no ELSE IF exists - nest an IF inside an ELSE
ELSE
{ ... }`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Pseudocode Challenge: Combining Conditions</h1>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Four problems on Boolean expressions.{" "}
            <code>AND</code>, <code>OR</code> and <code>NOT</code> are one of
            the few places the exam and Lua agree almost word for word &mdash;{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">almost</b>, and problem 3 is the exception.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Do this on paper.</b> There is nothing to run and nothing to
            type. On exam day you get this notation on a printed sheet and no
            computer, so practicing it any other way practices the wrong thing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="reference">
          <h2>Reference Card</h2>

          <p>
            Everything you need for this page. This is the same notation the
            College Board uses on the exam.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={RefCard}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Exam Notation — what you have so far"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Read <code>NOT</code> last and read it out loud.</b>{" "}
            <code>NOT (x &lt; y)</code> is not "x is greater than y" &mdash; it
            is "it is not the case that x is less than y", and the two are only
            the same answer when the values are different.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="problems">
          <h2>The Problems</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 1 &mdash; trace</h3>

          <p>What does this display?</p>

          <Code
            code={P1}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Problem 1"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b>Then answer this:</b> <code>hasPass</code> holds a value, and it
            is not being compared to anything. Say what kind of value it is and
            why <code>OR</code> accepts it on its own.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 2 &mdash; trace</h3>

          <p>
            Three separate <code>IF</code> statements, one after another. What
            does this display?
          </p>

          <Code
            code={P2}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Problem 2"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Not all three run. Not none of them run either.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 3 &mdash; Lua, and the one place this breaks</h3>

          <p>
            This is Lua, not exam notation.{" "}
            <b>Write down exactly what it prints</b>, then answer the question
            underneath.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={P3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="Problem 3"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The question:</b> the first{" "}
            <code>if</code> is handed the number <code>0</code>, not a
            comparison. Say what Lua does with it, and then say why{" "}
            <b>there is no way to write that first <code>if</code> in exam
            notation at all.</b>
          </p>

          <div class="alert alert-info">
            <b>Look at the reference card again.</b> <code>IF</code> takes a
            condition, and a condition on the exam is true or false. The sheet
            never says what <code>IF(0)</code> does{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">because on the exam that cannot happen</b>{" "}
            &mdash; and a habit you build in Lua will quietly follow you into
            May if nobody names it.
          </div>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 4 &mdash; trace</h3>

          <p>
            <code>RANDOM</code> does not give one answer, so write down{" "}
            <b>every value this could display</b>.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={P4}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Problem 4"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            The endpoints are the whole question. Check the reference card
            before you decide how many answers there are.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your four answers, plus the two short explanations from problems 1
            and 3. For the traces, write the output exactly as it appears,
            including spacing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Worth <b>3 points</b>, graded on completion. Show your working for
            the traces &mdash; a wrong answer with visible reasoning is worth
            more to both of us than a right one with none.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
